--TEST--
`object` is opened by `is` and by `as` onto the same object: a method call compiles inside either narrowing, both spellings answer the same thing, and a name the concrete class does not carry throws at the read — `rule:types/narrowing`, `rule:types/erased-member-access`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:classes/object-the-top-of-every-class-type
// A method call through `object` is refused "until `is` or `as` narrows it", and
// the refusal has its own case under `tests/conformance/reject/`. What is asked
// here is the other half: that both spellings open the call, that they open it
// onto one object, and that neither is needed to read a property.

class Tag {
    public function constructor(public string $text) {}

    public function shout(): string {
        return Core\Str::upper($this->text);
    }
}

class Plain {
    public function constructor(public string $text) {}
}

class Pick {
    public static function any(bool $tag): object {
        if ($tag) {
            return new Tag("x");
        }
        return new Plain("y");
    }
}

object $tagged = Pick::any(true);
object $plain = Pick::any(false);

// `rule:types/erased-member-access`: a property read needs no narrowing at all,
// because it is keyed on the written name and resolved at run time.
echo "read=", $tagged->text as string, ",", $plain->text as string, "\n";

// `rule:types/narrowing`: `is` narrows for the branch, so the call compiles inside it.
if ($tagged is Tag) {
    echo "is=", $tagged->shout(), "\n";
}

// `as` narrows into a named variable, which is then an ordinary `Tag`.
Tag $narrowed = $tagged as Tag;
echo "as=", $narrowed->shout(), "\n";

// The agreement, counted rather than read off two lines: the two spellings
// reached one object, so a narrowing that copied or re-created it fails here.
int $agreed = 0;
if ($tagged is Tag) {
    if ($tagged->shout() == $narrowed->shout()) {
        $agreed = $agreed + 1;
    }
    if ($tagged == $narrowed) {
        $agreed = $agreed + 1;
    }
    if ($tagged->text as string == $narrowed->text) {
        $agreed = $agreed + 1;
    }
}
echo "agreed=", $agreed, " of 3\n";

// `is` answers false for a class the object does not have, so the branch that
// would call the method is not entered.
int $entered = 0;
if ($plain is Tag) {
    $entered = $entered + 1;
}
echo "entered=", $entered, "\n";

// A name the concrete class does not carry throws at the read rather than
// answering anything.
try {
    mixed $absent = $plain->shout;
    echo "never reached\n";
} catch (Throwable $missing) {
    echo "missing name threw\n";
}
--EXPECT--
read=x,y
is=X
as=X
agreed=3 of 3
entered=0
missing name threw
